QtDMM Installation Instructions
(c) 2001–2014 M. Toussaint

QtDMM is a graphical front-end for digital multimeters based on Qt6 framework.


Requirements:
=============
- developer version of Qt 6, qserialport
- CMake version 3.16 or higher
- hidapi (ubuntu: libhidapi-devel, libhidapi-hidraw0 | fedora: hidapi-devel, hidapi | MacOSX/FreeBSD: hidapi )
- A supported or compatible multimeter


Compilation:
============
A helper script named `compile.sh` is included for convenience. You may use
it or follow the standard CMake workflow.

Basic steps:
------------
- Download and extract the QtDMM source code from GitHub:
  https://github.com/tuxmaster/QtDMM
- Open a terminal and change to the QtDMM directory:
    cd QtDMM

- For a clean build (optional):
    ./compile.sh clean <other options>

- Build and install system-wide:
    ./compile.sh install
or
- Build only (no installation):
    ./compile.sh

- The compiled binary will be placed in the `./bin` directory:
    ./bin/qtdmm

FreeBSD:
--------
- pkg install cmake ninja pkgconf qt6-base qt6-serialport qt6-charts qt6-tools hidapi
  (python3 for the documentation check in ctest). CMake-generated Makefiles
  need GNU make (gmake), so either install gmake or use -G Ninja as below.
- Build, test and install:
    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBUILD_TESTING=ON
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build
    sudo cmake --install build             (prefix /usr/local)
  compile.sh works as well when gmake is installed.
- Serial ports are /dev/cuaU0, /dev/cuau0 ... and belong to group dialer:
    sudo pw groupmod dialer -m $USER
- USB-HID cables are opened through libusb; give your user access to the
  ugen device node with a devfs.rules entry, e.g. in /etc/devfs.rules:
    [localrules=10]
    add path 'usb/*' mode 0660 group dialer
    add path 'ugen*' mode 0660 group dialer
  and devfs_system_ruleset="localrules" in /etc/rc.conf.

Windows:
--------
- Visual Studio 2019 or newer (Qt 6 does not support MSVC 2017) and a Qt 6.5+
  msvc2022_64 kit; hidapi is fetched and built automatically by CMake.
- From a "Developer Command Prompt" with Qt's bin directory in PATH:
    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBUILD_TESTING=ON
    cmake --build build
    ctest --test-dir build
    cpack --config build/CPackConfig.cmake      (optional: portable ZIP and
                                                 Inno Setup installer in packages/)
  or open the folder in Visual Studio ("Open a local folder", CMake project).
- Ready-made builds: every push to master produces a QtDMM-windows-x64
  artifact in the GitHub Actions "Windows build" workflow.

special installations:
----------------------

Arch Linux:
curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tuxmaster/QtDMM/refs/heads/master/PKGBUILD && makepkg --clean --syncdeps -fi

deb/rpm based Linux:
./compile.sh pack
will create a deb/rpm package. You can install this cleanly in your system.


Multimeter Compatibility:
=========================
If your DMM is not listed in the model selection (see README), try:
- Using manual configuration
- Selecting a similar model

Incorrect settings are unlikely to damage your device. In the worst case,
the multimeter may stop responding. Power-cycling it will usually fix this.


Feedback & Support:
===================
If you find working settings for an unsupported device or encounter problems,
please get in touch via the GitHub project page:

    https://github.com/tuxmaster/QtDMM

